{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UTy9HWVtynDhewW6uRXZzu","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Schnabel Lysmer Seed 1972 SHAKE earthquake response analysis horizontally layered sites UCB EERC report\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"SHAKE - A Computer Program for Earthquake Response Analysis of Horizontally Layered Sites | Resolution Copper Project and Land Exchange Environmental Impact Statement\",\"url\":\"https://www.resolutionmineeis.us/documents/schnabel-lysmer-seed-1972\"},{\"title\":\"Schnabel, P.B., Lysmer, J. and Seed, H.B. (1972) SHAKE —A computer program for earthquake response analysis of horizontal layered sites. Report. No. EERC 71-12, Earthquake Engineering Research Center, Berkeley. - References - Scientific Research Publishing\",\"url\":\"https://www.scirp.org/reference/referencespapers?referenceid=536307\"},{\"title\":\"P. B. Schnabel, J. Lysmer and H. B. Seed, “SHAKE, a Computer Program for Earthquake Response Analysis of Horizontally Layered Sites,” Report No. EERC 72-12, Earthquake Engineering Research Center, University of California, Berkeley, 1972. - References - Scientific Research Publishing\",\"url\":\"https://www.scirp.org/reference/ReferencesPapers?ReferenceID=881838\"},{\"title\":\"Schnabel, P.B., Lysmer, J. and Seed, H.B. (1972) SHAKE A Computer Program for Earthquake Response Analysis of Horizontally Layered Sites. Report No. UCB/ EERC-72/12, Earthquake Engineering Research Center, University of California, Berkeley, December, 102 p. - References - Scientific Research Publishing\",\"url\":\"https://www.scirp.org/reference/referencespapers?referenceid=2289131\"},{\"title\":\"The Earthquake Engineering Online Archive\",\"url\":\"https://nisee.berkeley.edu/elibrary/eerc/1972\"},{\"title\":\"Nrc\",\"url\":\"https://www.nrc.gov/docs/ML1125/ML11252A640.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"1972 Shake Schnabel and Seed | PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.scribd.com/document/364979048/1972-Shake-Schnabel-and-Seed\"},{\"title\":\"schnabel-lysmer-seed-1972.pdf\",\"url\":\"https://www.resolutionmineeis.us/sites/default/files/references/schnabel-lysmer-seed-1972.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Closure to “Procedure for Estimating Shear-Induced Seismic Slope Displacement for Shallow Crustal Earthquakes” by Jonathan D. Bray and Jorge Macedo | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 147, No 5\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002499\"},{\"title\":\"Earthquake Response Analysis of Embankment Dams | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-1-4613-0857-7_8\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found information about the publication you're looking for:\n\nThe report is \"SHAKE - A Computer Program for Earthquake Response Analysis of Horizontally Layered Sites\" by Schnabel, P.B., Lysmer, J. and Seed, H.B., published in 1972 by the Earthquake Engineering Research Center, University of California, Berkeley, Report No. EERC 72-12.\n\nThe report was published in December 1972 and contains 102 pages.\n\nSHAKE is a computer program for earthquake response analysis of horizontally layered sites developed at the University of California, Berkeley, by B. Schnabel, John Lysmer and H. B. Seed in 1972.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8dbc7cde-e70a-4bb0-94b0-45fd1a651a76","uuid":"83e58d69-fe43-45cc-8250-07ad4ddfbdf2","timestamp":"2026-03-29T13:37:22.209Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Schnabel Lysmer Seed 1972 SHAKE earthquake response analysis horizontally layered sites UCB EERC report","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01KLitADKjv3CXP25kBEy8Zz","content":[{"title":"SHAKE - A Computer Program for Earthquake Response Analysis of Horizontally Layered Sites | Resolution Copper Project and Land Exchange Environmental Impact Statement","url":"https://www.resolutionmineeis.us/documents/schnabel-lysmer-seed-1972"},{"title":"Schnabel, P.B., Lysmer, J. and Seed, H.B. (1972) SHAKE —A computer program for earthquake response analysis of horizontal layered sites. Report. No. EERC 71-12, Earthquake Engineering Research Center, Berkeley. - References - Scientific Research Publishing","url":"https://www.scirp.org/reference/referencespapers?referenceid=536307"},{"title":"P. B. Schnabel, J. Lysmer and H. B. Seed, “SHAKE, a Computer Program for Earthquake Response Analysis of Horizontally Layered Sites,” Report No. EERC 72-12, Earthquake Engineering Research Center, University of California, Berkeley, 1972. - References - Scientific Research Publishing","url":"https://www.scirp.org/reference/ReferencesPapers?ReferenceID=881838"},{"title":"Schnabel, P.B., Lysmer, J. and Seed, H.B. (1972) SHAKE A Computer Program for Earthquake Response Analysis of Horizontally Layered Sites. Report No. UCB/ EERC-72/12, Earthquake Engineering Research Center, University of California, Berkeley, December, 102 p. - References - Scientific Research Publishing","url":"https://www.scirp.org/reference/referencespapers?referenceid=2289131"},{"title":"The Earthquake Engineering Online Archive","url":"https://nisee.berkeley.edu/elibrary/eerc/1972"},{"title":"Nrc","url":"https://www.nrc.gov/docs/ML1125/ML11252A640.pdf"},{"title":"1972 Shake Schnabel and Seed | PDF","url":"https://www.scribd.com/document/364979048/1972-Shake-Schnabel-and-Seed"},{"title":"schnabel-lysmer-seed-1972.pdf","url":"https://www.resolutionmineeis.us/sites/default/files/references/schnabel-lysmer-seed-1972.pdf"},{"title":"Closure to “Procedure for Estimating Shear-Induced Seismic Slope Displacement for Shallow Crustal Earthquakes” by Jonathan D. Bray and Jorge Macedo | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 147, No 5","url":"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002499"},{"title":"Earthquake Response Analysis of Embankment Dams | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-1-4613-0857-7_8"}]},"Based on the web search results, I found information about the publication you're looking for:\n\nThe report is \"SHAKE - A Computer Program for Earthquake Response Analysis of Horizontally Layered Sites\" by Schnabel, P.B., Lysmer, J. and Seed, H.B., published in 1972 by the Earthquake Engineering Research Center, University of California, Berkeley, Report No. EERC 72-12.\n\nThe report was published in December 1972 and contains 102 pages.\n\nSHAKE is a computer program for earthquake response analysis of horizontally layered sites developed at the University of California, Berkeley, by B. Schnabel, John Lysmer and H. B. Seed in 1972."],"durationSeconds":3.903873707999999}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01E6QWBiDd7yfHak28HmFG6q","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"shear strain seismic site response integral acceleration depth \"rho\" closed form kinematic relationship 1D wave propagation\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Seismic Wave Propagation - an overview | ScienceDirect Topics\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/seismic-wave-propagation\"},{\"title\":\"Stochastic approach to study the site response in presence of shear wave velocity inversion: Application to seismic microzonation studies in Italy - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0013795220318111\"},{\"title\":\"A Review of Parameters and Methods for Seismic Site Response\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3263/15/4/128\"},{\"title\":\"Mathematical foundation of elastic wave propagation - SEG Wiki\",\"url\":\"https://wiki.seg.org/wiki/Mathematical_foundation_of_elastic_wave_propagation\"},{\"title\":\"Chapter 3 The Seismic Wave Equation\",\"url\":\"https://igppweb.ucsd.edu/~guy/sio227a/ch3.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction to the theory of seismic wave propagation\",\"url\":\"https://indico.ictp.it/event/a09169/session/34/contribution/19/material/0/0.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Nonlinear seismic response of the soil-structure system\",\"url\":\"https://theses.hal.science/tel-01282658v1/file/-CHANDRA_2014_diffusion.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"CHAPTER 1 2 Seismic Wave Propagation and Earth models\",\"url\":\"https://gfzpublic.gfz.de/pubman/item/item_4005_5/component/file_4006/Chapter_2_rev1.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Basics of wave propagation — GPG 0.0.1 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://gpg.geosci.xyz/content/seismic/wave_basics.html\"},{\"title\":\"Seismic Velocity — GPG 0.0.1 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://gpg.geosci.xyz/content/physical_properties/seismic_velocity_duplicate.html\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found several resources related to seismic site response and 1D wave propagation. Here's what the search revealed:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n1D seismic site response analyses rely on two major assumptions: all considered soil layers are horizontal and infinite, and dynamic response considers vertical propagation of horizontal shear waves.\n\nNumerical simulations of 1D site response are performed to evaluate ground shaking in terms of shear strain profiles and acceleration response spectra at the ground surface.\n\nImportant soil geotechnical properties for site response analysis include soil density (ρ), small strain shear modulus (Gmax) and the associated shear-wave velocity (VS), shear modulus reduction curve, damping ratio curve and Poisson's ratio.\n\nShear waves propagate by a pure shear strain perpendicular to the wave propagation.\n\nThe search results provide foundational information about 1D seismic wave propagation and site response analysis, including the role of soil density (rho) as a key parameter. However, the specific closed-form kinematic relationships involving integral acceleration and depth that you're asking about are not directly detailed in these particular search results. The results focus more on numerical methods and general wave propagation theory rather than specific closed-form mathematical relationships relating these parameters.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8dbc7cde-e70a-4bb0-94b0-45fd1a651a76","uuid":"752c9718-e44f-4cae-97bc-93f0660dcfc1","timestamp":"2026-03-29T13:37:25.093Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"shear strain seismic site response integral acceleration depth \"rho\" closed form kinematic relationship 1D wave propagation","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01KbzF3mYM2qH8543CuD8jsN","content":[{"title":"Seismic Wave Propagation - an overview | ScienceDirect Topics","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/seismic-wave-propagation"},{"title":"Stochastic approach to study the site response in presence of shear wave velocity inversion: Application to seismic microzonation studies in Italy - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0013795220318111"},{"title":"A Review of Parameters and Methods for Seismic Site Response","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3263/15/4/128"},{"title":"Mathematical foundation of elastic wave propagation - SEG Wiki","url":"https://wiki.seg.org/wiki/Mathematical_foundation_of_elastic_wave_propagation"},{"title":"Chapter 3 The Seismic Wave Equation","url":"https://igppweb.ucsd.edu/~guy/sio227a/ch3.pdf"},{"title":"Introduction to the theory of seismic wave propagation","url":"https://indico.ictp.it/event/a09169/session/34/contribution/19/material/0/0.pdf"},{"title":"Nonlinear seismic response of the soil-structure system","url":"https://theses.hal.science/tel-01282658v1/file/-CHANDRA_2014_diffusion.pdf"},{"title":"CHAPTER 1 2 Seismic Wave Propagation and Earth models","url":"https://gfzpublic.gfz.de/pubman/item/item_4005_5/component/file_4006/Chapter_2_rev1.pdf"},{"title":"Basics of wave propagation — GPG 0.0.1 documentation","url":"https://gpg.geosci.xyz/content/seismic/wave_basics.html"},{"title":"Seismic Velocity — GPG 0.0.1 documentation","url":"https://gpg.geosci.xyz/content/physical_properties/seismic_velocity_duplicate.html"}]},"Based on the web search results, I found several resources related to seismic site response and 1D wave propagation. Here's what the search revealed:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n1D seismic site response analyses rely on two major assumptions: all considered soil layers are horizontal and infinite, and dynamic response considers vertical propagation of horizontal shear waves.\n\nNumerical simulations of 1D site response are performed to evaluate ground shaking in terms of shear strain profiles and acceleration response spectra at the ground surface.\n\nImportant soil geotechnical properties for site response analysis include soil density (ρ), small strain shear modulus (Gmax) and the associated shear-wave velocity (VS), shear modulus reduction curve, damping ratio curve and Poisson's ratio.\n\nShear waves propagate by a pure shear strain perpendicular to the wave propagation.\n\nThe search results provide foundational information about 1D seismic wave propagation and site response analysis, including the role of soil density (rho) as a key parameter. However, the specific closed-form kinematic relationships involving integral acceleration and depth that you're asking about are not directly detailed in these particular search results. The results focus more on numerical methods and general wave propagation theory rather than specific closed-form mathematical relationships relating these parameters."],"durationSeconds":5.993631374999997}}
