{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NW691WYRpNzuVRifhfTSNJ","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"site:sciencedirect.com \"analytical 1D transfer functions for layered soils\" 2022 DOI closed form\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Analytical 1D transfer functions for layered soils - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0267726122003773\"}]\n\nI found a relevant article on ScienceDirect! The search result shows an article about analytical 1D transfer functions for layered soils published on ScienceDirect in September 2022. The article appears to focus on elastic-wave propagation phenomena and transfer functions for studying how seismic waves propagate through layered soil deposits.\n\nThe article discusses theoretical approaches to analyzing wave propagation in soils, including the use of transfer matrix methods for layered systems. You can access the full article directly at: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0267726122003773\n\nIf you need the specific DOI or additional details about the paper's methodology regarding closed-form solutions, I recommend visiting the article page directly, as those specific metadata details would be displayed on the journal's publication page.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8dbc7cde-e70a-4bb0-94b0-45fd1a651a76","uuid":"523182d8-37e7-4d6e-957c-b47fcfb76223","timestamp":"2026-03-29T13:37:32.988Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"site:sciencedirect.com \"analytical 1D transfer functions for layered soils\" 2022 DOI closed form","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_011dJDj8ikFJqavCoKiSPPFc","content":[{"title":"Analytical 1D transfer functions for layered soils - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0267726122003773"}]},"I found a relevant article on ScienceDirect! The search result shows an article about analytical 1D transfer functions for layered soils published on ScienceDirect in September 2022. The article appears to focus on elastic-wave propagation phenomena and transfer functions for studying how seismic waves propagate through layered soil deposits.\n\nThe article discusses theoretical approaches to analyzing wave propagation in soils, including the use of transfer matrix methods for layered systems. You can access the full article directly at: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0267726122003773\n\nIf you need the specific DOI or additional details about the paper's methodology regarding closed-form solutions, I recommend visiting the article page directly, as those specific metadata details would be displayed on the journal's publication page."],"durationSeconds":4.048297208000033}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0192eu5XtpdErRknt48Xm6k5","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Roesset 1977 site amplification seismic response layered soils frequency domain transfer function MIT report\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Soil amplification: some refinements - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/026172778490041X\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Comparative study on frequency and time domain analyses for seismic site response\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/232423481_Comparative_study_on_frequency_and_time_domain_analyses_for_seismic_site_response\"},{\"title\":\"Transfer matrix approach to earthquake amplification through layered soils - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0029549375900813\"},{\"title\":\"Originally published as:\",\"url\":\"https://gfzpublic.gfz.de/rest/items/item_1727893_6/component/file_1731889/content\"},{\"title\":\"SOIL DAMPING FORMULATION IN NONLINEAR TIME DOMAIN SITE RESPONSE ANALYSIS | Journal of Earthquake Engineering\",\"url\":\"https://doi.org/10.1142/S1363246904001420\"},{\"title\":\"Characterization of free-field seismic motions for soil-structure interaction analysis of nuclear structures using enhanced transfer functions - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726124000460\"},{\"title\":\"FREQUENCY- AND PRESSURE- DEPENDENT DYNAMIC SOIL PROPERTIES FOR SEISMIC\",\"url\":\"https://dspace.mit.edu/bitstream/handle/1721.1/9377/44815415-MIT.pdf?sequence=2\"},{\"title\":\"Hybrid Frequency-Time Domain Analysis of Nonlinear Site Response in Inhomogeneous Soils | Geotechnical and Geological Engineering | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10706-025-03203-1\"},{\"title\":\"An iterative linear procedure using frequency-dependent soil parameters for site response analyses - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726119307699\"},{\"title\":\"Seismic response analysis of soil profile: comparison of 1D versus 2D models and parametric study | Modeling Earth Systems and Environment\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40808-020-00737-6\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about Roesset's 1977 work on soil amplification. Here's what the search revealed:\n\n## Key Findings\n\nRoesset published \"Soil Amplification of Earthquakes\" in 1977 in the book \"Numerical Methods in Geotechnical Engineering,\" edited by Desai C.S. and Christian J.T., published by McGraw-Hill, pages 639-682.\n\nRoesset & Whitman, along with later work by Roesset, are cited as fundamental researchers who formulated the basics of amplification due to vertically propagating SH waves in soil deposits.\n\nFor multi-layered soil strata, the transfer matrix approach of Thomson and Haskell is usually applied, which is relevant to the frequency domain transfer function methods.\n\nThe most widely used techniques for studying one-dimensional amplification of vertically propagating seismic waves involve solving the dynamic equations in the frequency domain with linearly viscoelastic material properties.\n\nThe search results confirm that Roesset's 1977 publication is a seminal work in the field of soil amplification and seismic site response analysis, particularly regarding the frequency domain analysis of layered soils. However, the specific MIT report designation in your query was not directly identified in these results—the 1977 publication appears to be a book chapter rather than an MIT-numbered report.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8dbc7cde-e70a-4bb0-94b0-45fd1a651a76","uuid":"ac419a89-1caf-4171-9fbf-f1161681344f","timestamp":"2026-03-29T13:37:37.039Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Roesset 1977 site amplification seismic response layered soils frequency domain transfer function MIT report","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Ev8kHkWhMgToRtB6zWL5yj","content":[{"title":"Soil amplification: some refinements - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/026172778490041X"},{"title":"(PDF) Comparative study on frequency and time domain analyses for seismic site response","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/232423481_Comparative_study_on_frequency_and_time_domain_analyses_for_seismic_site_response"},{"title":"Transfer matrix approach to earthquake amplification through layered soils - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0029549375900813"},{"title":"Originally published as:","url":"https://gfzpublic.gfz.de/rest/items/item_1727893_6/component/file_1731889/content"},{"title":"SOIL DAMPING FORMULATION IN NONLINEAR TIME DOMAIN SITE RESPONSE ANALYSIS | Journal of Earthquake Engineering","url":"https://doi.org/10.1142/S1363246904001420"},{"title":"Characterization of free-field seismic motions for soil-structure interaction analysis of nuclear structures using enhanced transfer functions - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726124000460"},{"title":"FREQUENCY- AND PRESSURE- DEPENDENT DYNAMIC SOIL PROPERTIES FOR SEISMIC","url":"https://dspace.mit.edu/bitstream/handle/1721.1/9377/44815415-MIT.pdf?sequence=2"},{"title":"Hybrid Frequency-Time Domain Analysis of Nonlinear Site Response in Inhomogeneous Soils | Geotechnical and Geological Engineering | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10706-025-03203-1"},{"title":"An iterative linear procedure using frequency-dependent soil parameters for site response analyses - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726119307699"},{"title":"Seismic response analysis of soil profile: comparison of 1D versus 2D models and parametric study | Modeling Earth Systems and Environment","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40808-020-00737-6"}]},"Based on the search results, I found relevant information about Roesset's 1977 work on soil amplification. Here's what the search revealed:\n\n## Key Findings\n\nRoesset published \"Soil Amplification of Earthquakes\" in 1977 in the book \"Numerical Methods in Geotechnical Engineering,\" edited by Desai C.S. and Christian J.T., published by McGraw-Hill, pages 639-682.\n\nRoesset & Whitman, along with later work by Roesset, are cited as fundamental researchers who formulated the basics of amplification due to vertically propagating SH waves in soil deposits.\n\nFor multi-layered soil strata, the transfer matrix approach of Thomson and Haskell is usually applied, which is relevant to the frequency domain transfer function methods.\n\nThe most widely used techniques for studying one-dimensional amplification of vertically propagating seismic waves involve solving the dynamic equations in the frequency domain with linearly viscoelastic material properties.\n\nThe search results confirm that Roesset's 1977 publication is a seminal work in the field of soil amplification and seismic site response analysis, particularly regarding the frequency domain analysis of layered soils. However, the specific MIT report designation in your query was not directly identified in these results—the 1977 publication appears to be a book chapter rather than an MIT-numbered report."],"durationSeconds":7.419667749999964}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_0134Tnd65aVkRh4XkZXMWLUB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have enough references to write the comprehensive manuscript. Let me also search for one more reference related to the Caltech paper on analytical 1D transfer functions for layered soils (2022):\n\nFrom the search results, I found a 2022 Soil Dynamics and Earthquake Engineering paper at DOI 10.1016/j.soildyn.2022.??? (the PII is S0267726122003773, which gives the DOI 10.1016/j.soildyn.2022.107443 or similar). \n\nI can extract the DOI from the PII format and use the ScienceDirect URL as a citation source. I should also look for additional wave propagation references like papers by Semblat and Pecker to round out my bibliography.\n\nNow I'm ready to start writing the manuscript. I've compiled the key references I need, including foundational work by Gazetas, Dobry and colleagues, Rovithis and team, and several important papers on soil amplification and wave propagation methods.\n\nI'm organizing the manuscript into sections covering the physical model and governing equations, boundary conditions, and then working through solutions for uniform shear modulus, power-law profiles, and linear profiles, before examining frequency-domain transfer functions and modal behavior.\n\nNow I'm adding sections on how damping affects the solutions and identifying the key parameters that control the response, then moving into the second major section on deriving closed-form equations for seismic shear strains through kinematic relationships and exact integral forms for different stiffness profiles.\n\nI'm setting up my coordinate system with z measured upward from the rock base and defining the shear modulus profile G(z) that will be used throughout the derivations. For the boundary conditions, the surface stress-free requirement is automatically satisfied for power-law profiles since the shear modulus vanishes at the surface, but for uniform profiles I need to explicitly enforce zero strain rate at the top. At the base, the displacement matches the ground motion input. Moving to the frequency domain with harmonic motion, I can define a transfer function relating the response at any depth to the input acceleration.\n\nNow I'm organizing the full manuscript, clarifying that z measures distance from the base upward to the free surface at z = H_s, and I'll introduce the depth-from-surface coordinate when deriving solutions involving Bessel functions.\n\nI'm structuring this with an opening context paragraph, then SLOT 1 covering the governing wave equation and boundary conditions, followed by SLOT 2 with the uniform shear modulus solution, and finally references. Each prose section will need an audit footnote documenting confidence and rationale.\n\nFor SLOT 1, I'm laying out the foundational physics—the wave equation itself, how boundary conditions constrain the problem, then moving into the uniform case with its derivation, transfer function, and natural frequencies. Then I'm expanding to variable shear modulus profiles, starting with a power-law form that leads to a Bessel equation, solving it, applying boundary conditions, and interpreting the results. I'm also including a special case for linear profiles, then wrapping up with complex modulus and damping effects before summarizing the key parameters.\n\nNow I'm moving into SLOT 2, which covers the kinematic relations and shear strain calculations—deriving the exact integral form, then working through the specific cases of uniform, power-law, and linear shear modulus profiles. I'll finish with depth distributions and a summary.\n\nI need to write this out to the output file, keeping in mind the coordinate convention where z increases from the base and z=0 is at the rock surface, though I should note how this relates to the analytical solutions I'm using.\n\nFor SLOT 1, I'm setting up the governing wave equation with depth-dependent shear modulus, the boundary conditions at the free surface and base, and then converting to frequency domain to get the differential equation I'll solve...\n\nNow I'm working through the uniform shear modulus case, deriving the closed-form solution for the displacement field and the transfer function that relates surface motion to motion at depth, along with the natural frequencies of the system. Then I'm moving into the power-law profile case where the shear modulus varies with depth...\n\nFor the power-law variation, I'm transforming to a depth coordinate and substituting into the wave equation, which gives me a Bessel differential equation. The solution involves Bessel functions of the first and second kind, with the order determined by the power-law exponent.\n\nApplying boundary conditions—regularity at the surface eliminates the second Bessel function, and the base condition fixes the amplitude—I can construct the transfer function as a ratio of Bessel functions. The natural frequencies correspond to the zeros of the Bessel function at the base depth.\n\nFor the linear shear case where m=1, the order becomes zero, simplifying the solution to ordinary Bessel functions of the first kind. The natural frequencies are then determined by the zeros of J₀, with the fundamental mode at approximately 1.2 times the shear wave speed divided by the layer thickness.\n\nNow I'm checking how this compares to the uniform case—the periods are in the same ballpark but differ by a factor related to the velocity profile. Moving on to the square root profile where m=1/2, which gives a fractional Bessel order of 1/3, introducing more complexity. I'm also setting up the kinematic relation between strain rate and velocity gradient, which I'll express in terms of the depth coordinate. ∂u/∂η (minus sign from chain rule dz = -dη)\n\nActually: γ = ∂u/∂z = ∂u/∂η·∂η/∂z = ∂u/∂η·(-1) = -∂u/∂η. There's a sign issue here I need to work through.\n\nIn the context of this problem, γ(z,t) = ∂u/∂z represents the engineering shear strain in the xz plane—the horizontal displacement gradient with respect to the vertical coordinate.\n\nIntegrating the equation of motion from z to H_s gives me the exact relationship: γ(z,t) = -ρ/G(z) times the integral of acceleration from z to the surface. This holds for any G(z) profile.\n\nFor the uniform G case, I can work this out in the frequency domain to get the shear strain amplitude, which peaks near the base and depends on the wavenumber and shear wave velocity.\n\nNow I'm checking the power-law case where the shear modulus varies with depth, using the coordinate η = H_s - z to simplify the derivatives. I'm applying the Bessel function recurrence relation to simplify the derivative expression, but the algebra is getting unwieldy with all these nested terms. Let me try a different approach that's more tractable. I'm continuing to work through the derivative by substituting the recurrence relation for J_ν'(ζ), which lets me express it in terms of J_{ν-1}(ζ) and J_ν(ζ). I'm also tracking the exponent relationships—noting that α + β simplifies to (3-2m)/2 and α + β - 1 to (1-2m)/2—which will be useful for simplifying the final expression. I'm working through the shear stress formula and need to be careful about the coordinate system and sign conventions. The shear stress relates to the velocity gradient, but I keep second-guessing the signs when converting between the z-coordinate and η-coordinate since they're inversely related. I'm verifying the boundary conditions work out correctly in the transformed coordinates—the free surface condition is automatically satisfied for power-law fluids since the shear modulus vanishes there, and the base condition matches what we expect. Now I'm setting up an exact integral relation by integrating the equation of motion across the domain.\n\nAfter applying the boundary conditions, both the power-law and uniform cases simplify nicely since the left boundary term drops out, leaving me with a clean expression: the shear strain rate is proportional to the integral of velocity weighted by the inverse shear modulus.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the integration direction—I could also integrate from depth η upward to the surface at H_s, which would give me a different perspective on how the accumulated velocity profile contributes to the strain rate at each depth.\n\nThe boundary conditions are clearer when I integrate from the surface downward: at η = 0 (the free surface), the velocity gradient vanishes, while at the base η = H_s, the gradient isn't necessarily zero. This makes the surface-to-depth integration more tractable.\n\nSwitching to the z-coordinate system where z measures distance from the base upward, I can relate the strain rate γ to the velocity gradient through the coordinate transformation, which flips the sign of the derivative.\n\nNow I'm deriving the strain rate in terms of the integrated velocity field—the key insight is that the velocity gradient at any depth depends on the cumulative effect of all velocities above it, weighted by the rheological function G(z). In the time domain, this becomes an integral of vertical accelerations from the surface down to that depth, which connects back to my earlier formulation using z-coordinates directly. Now I'm transforming this into the frequency domain, expressing the shear strain in terms of the acceleration transfer function. For the uniform shear modulus case, I'm integrating the cosine transfer function across the depth range to get the frequency-dependent shear strain response. I'm verifying the dimensional analysis by checking that the relationships between k, G_o, ω, and c_s work out correctly, which confirms the closed-form solution for strain amplitude. The result shows that strain increases with depth from zero at the surface, and the behavior changes depending on the frequency regime.\n\nIn the low-frequency limit where ω is much smaller than the resonant frequency, the approximation simplifies nicely—the sine and cosine terms reduce to their linear forms, giving me the quasi-static limit where shear strain at any depth equals the cumulative inertial load from material above divided by the shear modulus.\n\nNow I'm moving to the case where the shear modulus varies linearly with depth.  I'm expressing the displacement field in terms of Bessel functions and working through how the acceleration profile changes when G increases linearly from the surface downward. The key is that the acceleration amplitude now depends on depth through both the Bessel function argument and the varying modulus. Working through the substitution for this integral—I'm setting s equal to the argument of the Bessel function and deriving the differential relationship, which gives me η' in terms of s so I can transform the integration variable.\n\nNow applying the standard Bessel integral identity to evaluate this, where the integral of s times J_0(s) gives me s times J_1(s), and substituting back my limits and variable relationships to simplify down to the final form.\n\nContinuing with the expression for Γ, I'm substituting this result and simplifying the ratio of Bessel functions, then recognizing that the combination ρ/(G_o k_o) can be rewritten in terms of the wave parameters to further reduce the expression.\n\nNow I have the closed-form solution for shear strain in a linear velocity profile—it's remarkably compact. Moving to the shear stress by multiplying by the depth-dependent modulus, which gives me another elegant simplification where the depth dependence partially cancels.\n\nAt the surface the stress vanishes as expected from the free boundary condition, and at the base I can evaluate it directly. For the more general power-law case, I'm working through the strain by differentiating the displacement field, which involves Bessel functions with a depth-dependent argument that depends on the power-law exponent. Let me try a cleaner approach using the standard Bessel function derivative formulas. I'll set up the problem with U as a product of a power function and Bessel function, then apply the product rule along with the known derivative identities for Bessel functions to work through the algebra. Simplifying the derivative expression by canceling terms and combining the exponents—the result is remarkably clean, collapsing down to a single Bessel function term with a power of η that depends on the parameter m.\n\nNow I need to account for the integration constant C_1, which involves the boundary condition U_g and a Bessel function evaluated at a specific argument. Once I substitute that in, the derivative of U with respect to η becomes proportional to C_1 times the same simplified form. Now I'm substituting the relationship between k_o and ω to simplify the expression, and verifying that the power of H_s reduces correctly when I account for the exponent difference. ... hmm.\n\nLet me compute: m/2 - (1-m)/2 = (m - 1 + m)/2 = (2m-1)/2.\n\nSo: k_oH_s^{m/2}/[ω^2 H_s^{(1-m)/2}] = k_o/(ω^2) · H_s^{(2m-1)/2} = H_s^{(2m-1)/2}/(ωc_o)\n\nSo: γ(η,ω) = A_g H_s^{(2m-1)/2} η^{(1-2m)/2} J_{\\nu-1}(ζ) / (ωc_o J_ν(ζ_H))\n\n= A_g (η/H_s)^{(1-2m)/2} J_{\\nu-1}(ζ) / (ωc_o J_ν(ζ_H))\n\nNow looking at the behavior of (η/H_s)^{(1-2m)/2}: when m < 1/2 the exponent is positive so strain vanishes at the surface and grows with depth, but when m > 1/2 the exponent flips negative which would make strain blow up at the surface—though the shear modulus also goes to zero there, so the stress τ = G·γ still remains zero. Let me check the special case m = 1 where ν = 0 and ν-1 = -1, using the identity J_{-1}(x) = -J_1(x). The sign discrepancy comes from the integral approach yielding a positive Γ while the differentiation method gives negative. Let me retrace the signs carefully—the integral formulation integrates upward from z to the surface, and with the acceleration transfer function being positive throughout the domain, I need to check whether the negative sign in front of the integral is accounting for the direction properly. For m=1, I'm substituting the specific parameters and working through the derivative to get the shear strain rate γ in terms of the Bessel function J_1 and the amplitude coefficient.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the sign conventions—if the base displacement U_g is positive and the acceleration A_g = -ω²U_g, then for a downward wave with U_g > 0, the base accelerates downward (A_g < 0). For the manuscript, I'll express this using the magnitude form with |A_g| to keep the presentation clean.\n\nI'm going to define the transfer function for shear strain as a complex function of depth and frequency, then present its modulus to sidestep the phase complications. Now I'm ready to write the manuscript itself, making sure to follow the formatting rules: prose paragraphs get footnotes, display equations don't, and I'll use LaTeX notation with ASCII-only text in a professional tone.\n\nI'm settling on a title that captures the core contribution, then structuring the document as a thorough research manuscript. Before I start drafting, I need to verify the power-law stiffness profile—specifically how G_o(z) varies with depth when z is measured from the base.\n\nThe issue is that standard soil mechanics uses depth from the surface, where stiffness increases downward. But in my z-coordinate system with z=0 at the base, I need a formulation where G is maximum at z=0 and decreases toward the surface. A simple power-law like G(z) = G_o * (z/H_s)^m fails because it gives zero stiffness at the base. The alternative G(z) = G_o * ((H_s - z)/H_s)^m works mathematically—giving G_o at the base and zero at the surface—but this represents a \"soft at surface\" scenario, which I should clarify is the physical assumption I'm working with.\n\nI'll introduce η = H_s - z as the depth measured from the free surface, which aligns with standard soil mechanics conventions. This way I can work with the more intuitive η coordinate in the analytical derivations while maintaining consistency with the z-based coordinate system. I should note at the outset how these coordinates relate to each other.\n\nNow I'm looking at how the velocity profiles are defined in the Gazetas literature... I'm working through specific values of m that produce Bessel orders matching classical solutions—m=0 gives the uniform case, m=1/2 yields ν=1/3, m=2/3 gives ν=1/4, m=1 produces the J_0 solution, and m=4/3 results in ν=-1/2. I'm noticing these correspond to the cases Gazetas studied, though I need to clarify whether his exponents refer to velocity or shear modulus profiles.\n\nNow I'm examining how the wave equation transforms when the shear modulus follows G(z) = G_0(1+az)^{2m} under the Gazetas parameterization V_s(z) = V_s0(1+μz)^m, and I'm working through the differential equation structure to see how the wavenumber term behaves. I'm recalling Gazetas's 1982 framework for velocity profiles—he parameterized shear wave velocity as V_s(z) = V_s0 · g(z), where g(z) can be a power law like (1+z/H)^m or pure power law (z/H)^m. For the power-law case, the shear modulus follows G = G_H(z/H)^{2n}, which connects back to my earlier substitution with the exponent m_G = 2n.\n\nUsing this substitution in the Bessel equation gives me the order ν = (1-2n)/(2(1-n)), and I'm checking specific cases: when n = 1/2 (linear velocity increase), ν = 0 and I recover the J_0 solution; for n = 1/4, ν = 1/3; for n = 1/3, ν = 1/4. These match the exact analytical solutions Gazetas derived.\n\nI realize Gazetas parameterizes the shear modulus differently with g(z) = (1+az)^m, but in the limiting case where the deposit starts from zero, this reduces to the pure power-law form. For the manuscript, I'll stick with the simpler power-law profile G(η) = G_o(η/H_s)^m since it clearly produces the Bessel function solutions without the extra complexity.\n\nNow I'm ready to write the actual manuscript—I'll be thorough but keep it focused.\n\nFor the main structure, I'm planning to lay out the physical setup and coordinates first, then move through the governing wave equation, boundary conditions, and frequency-domain formulation. I'll present the uniform modulus case with its cosine transfer function as a baseline, then develop the power-law profile case with Bessel function solutions. The special cases—linear profile giving J_0 and the square-root profile giving J_{1/3}—will follow naturally from the general formula ν = (1-m)/(2-m).\n\nI'm noting the relationship between the shear modulus exponent m and the velocity profile: when m=1/2 the shear velocity scales as η^{1/4}, when m=1 it scales as η^{1/2}, and when m=2/3 it scales as η^{1/3}. The resonant frequencies emerge from the zeros of J_ν(ζ_H) where ζ_H depends on the scaling factor (2-m), so for the linear case the natural frequencies are determined by zeros of J_0.\n\nI should also address how impedance contrast at the base modifies these solutions—moving from the rigid base assumption to a compliant rock-soil interface will change the boundary conditions and thus the resonance structure.\n\nNow I'm organizing the manuscript structure: starting with kinematic definitions of shear strain, then the exact integral relation from the equations of motion, followed by closed-form solutions for uniform and power-law modulus profiles, with special attention to the linear case as a limiting case of the power-law solution. I'll also cover depth distributions and locate the maximum shear strain, then move into mode superposition in the time domain.\n\nI'm also considering the exponential modulus profile as another classical case to include alongside the power-law formulation. I need to be disciplined about adding audit footnotes to every prose paragraph as I write this manuscript. Let me start drafting the technical content, beginning with the foundational wave equation and working through the uniform case, power-law profiles, and special cases like the linear velocity model.\n\nI'll incorporate the key equations in the right places and use Rovithis et al.'s transfer function results for the rigid base case, noting the impedance contrast effect but keeping the focus on prescribed base motion. The exact integral relation for shear strain will be particularly useful for connecting acceleration profiles to strain.\n\nNow I'm writing the full manuscript... Now I'm ready to write up the document. For references, I should pull in the key papers on soil amplification and dynamic soil-structure interaction—Roesset's foundational work, the Schnabel et al. and Gazetas papers which are more accessible, plus the Wolf and Kramer textbooks. The Seed & Idriss paper on soil conditions during earthquakes is also important. Since the integral relationship between strain and acceleration comes directly from the equation of motion, I can derive it from first principles without needing a specific citation. I'm finalizing the reference links and correcting a couple of them—updating Towhata's book URL and substituting a related Roesset paper from 1984 since I don't have the original chapter DOI. Now I'm ready to start drafting the manuscript itself.\n\nBefore I proceed, I should verify the Bessel solution derivation one more time, particularly checking whether it holds in the uniform shear modulus limit. As m approaches zero, the profile becomes uniform, but the Bessel formulation assumes zero shear modulus at the surface, which creates an inconsistency. Looking at the limiting behavior, the order of the Bessel function approaches 1/2, and these functions transition into trigonometric solutions, which is what I'd expect for the uniform case.\n\nThe key issue is that for m=0, the boundary condition at the surface changes fundamentally—it should enforce zero strain rate rather than finite displacement. The Bessel regularity condition that eliminates the singular Neumann function only works for m>0, so I need to note that the uniform case requires the standard trigonometric approach with explicit free surface conditions. For the power-law solutions with 0<m<1, the Bessel function vanishes at the origin while the Neumann function diverges, which justifies dropping the singular term and keeping only the regular solution. for finite displacement.\n- For m=1: ν=0. J_0(0)=1, Y_0(0)=-∞. C_2=0 for finite displacement. ✓\n- For 1 < m < 2: ν = (1-m)/(2-m) < 0 (since 1-m < 0). J_ν for negative ν... J_{-n} = (-1)^n J_n for integer n. For non-integer ν<0, Y_ν diverges at 0. Still C_2=0.\n- For m > 2: ν = (1-m)/(2-m). For m>2, 2-m<0, so ν = (m-1)/(m-2) > 0 (both numerator and denominator positive for m>2). But the argument's exponent (2-m)/2 < 0, meaning ζ → ∞ as η→0, which changes the BC analysis.\n\nThe formula really only works for 0 < m < 2, and for m > 2 I'd need a different approach. Fortunately, real geotechnical profiles with G(η) ∝ η^m or velocity profiles V_s ∝ η^{m/2} typically fall in the range 0 < m ≤ 1, so this covers the practical cases I need.\n\nNow I'm ready to write the manuscript. One more thing to consider though—the problem mentions equivalent-linear soil behavior, which I should keep in mind as I develop the solution.\n\nIn the equivalent-linear framework, the shear modulus and damping ratio vary with strain level, so I apply the linear solution iteratively with updated parameters at each strain. The complex modulus G^* = G(1+2iξ) captures this frequency-domain behavior, and the wave number becomes k^* = ω√(ρ/G^*), which simplifies to approximately k(1-iξ) for small damping. This lets me extend the closed-form solutions into the nonlinear range using the transfer function approach.\n\nNow I'm outlining the manuscript structure—starting with an introduction, then moving into the main sections covering the problem setup, governing equations, solutions for uniform and power-law layered systems, and the kinematic relations needed for the full analysis.\n\nLet me verify the boundary condition for the