 I relied on for $m>0$. Instead, I need to enforce the free surface condition $dU/d\\xi=0$ directly. This means the Bessel function approach I derived is really only valid for $m>0$, where the profile softens at the surface, while the trigonometric formula handles the uniform case separately. The two approaches are consistent: the general Bessel framework covers $0<m<2$, and $m=0$ branches off as its own special case.\n\nFor intermediate values like $m=0.5$, I get $\\nu=1/3$, which involves non-elementary Bessel functions like $J_{-2/3}$. So for the manuscript, the main cases worth highlighting are the uniform profile at $m=0$ with trigonometric solutions, the linear profile at $m=1$ with standard $J_0/J_1$ Bessel functions, and the general range $0<m<2$ with the full $J_\\nu/J_{\\nu-1}$ framework. I should also clarify that $G_o$ is the maximum value at the base and decreases toward the surface, with the depth profile given by $G(\\xi)=G_o(\\xi/H_s)^m$ vanishing at the surface.\n\nNow I need to express everything in the task's coordinate system where $z$ is measured from the base upward. Converting from the depth coordinate $\\xi=H_s-z$, the profile becomes $G(z)=G_o((H_s-z)/H_s)^m$, which correctly gives $G_o$ at the base and zero at the surface. I'll need to transform the solution formula accordingly using this coordinate change. For the linear profile, I'm deriving the shear strain amplitude by taking the derivative with respect to the normalized depth coordinate, which gives me the strain response in terms of Bessel functions and the ground motion amplitude. I'm working through the general form for power-law velocity profiles, expressing the reflection coefficient in terms of depth from the surface η instead of absolute depth z to simplify the notation and make the relationships clearer.\n\nNow I'm ready to structure the full manuscript with sections covering the physical model, governing equations, boundary conditions, and solutions for uniform, linear, and power-law stiffness profiles, followed by the kinematic relationships and strain calculations. I need to make sure I'm using the task's coordinate convention where $z$ is measured from the base, not from the surface. The power-law relationship is $G(z) = G_o(1 - z/H_s)^m$ with maximum stiffness $G_o$ at the base, and I'll introduce depth from surface $\\eta = H_s - z$ as a mathematical convenience but present final results in the task's $z$ coordinates.\n\nFor the stiffness profile to have its maximum at the base as stated, I need $m > 0$, which gives the classic Gibson soil model where stiffness decreases toward the surface. The parameter $m$ controls how rapidly this decrease occurs—small values produce gentle gradients while larger values create steeper transitions.\n\nNow I'm clarifying the notation: $G_o$ represents the maximum stiffness value at the base, and $G(z)$ is the profile function itself. With that settled, I should move forward with writing the manuscript.\n\nI'm also reconsidering the integral relationship for strain in terms of depth measured from the base. Let me carefully re-derive the equation of motion and work through how acceleration integrates to give strain.\n\nDeriving the strain-acceleration relationship...\n\nI'm finding that the shear strain depends on integrating acceleration from depth z up to the surface, and remarkably, this result holds regardless of the shear modulus profile. The shear stress follows the same pattern—it's determined entirely by the acceleration field and density, independent of how G varies with depth. Now I'm ready to write up these findings.\n\nI'm organizing the manuscript into two main sections following the workflow structure. The first section covers closed-form analytical solutions for seismic site acceleration response, with subsections on the physical model, wave equation, boundary conditions, transfer functions for different shear modulus profiles, natural frequencies, and damping effects.\n\nThe second section addresses closed-form equations for seismic shear strains derived from the acceleration field, including kinematic relationships, integral representations, and strain calculations across different profile types.\n\nNow I need to write out the full text carefully, making sure each prose paragraph includes an audit footnote with confidence levels and rationale, while keeping display math blocks footnote-free.\n\nFor citations, I'm using DOI and web references for Gazetas, Dobry et al., Rovithis et al., and the Schnabel-Lysmer-Seed documentation.  I'll also include Towhata's textbook and the recent Rovithis 2022 paper on 1D transfer functions via web links. Since the knowledge base is empty, I need to ensure I have at least three authoritative external citations to properly support the manuscript.\n\nNow I'm ready to write the full document. I'll use $z$ as the vertical coordinate measured upward from the base, and introduce $\\eta = H_s - z$ as depth from the free surface for the mathematical derivations, then present final formulas in terms of $z$ to match the task requirements. For the formatting, display equations like the amplitude response function will be centered on their own lines with blank spacing before and after, with the preceding paragraph ending at its footnote before the equation block begins. Now I'm setting up the physical model for a saturated soil layer overlying bedrock, defining the coordinate system with the base at $z = 0$ and the surface at $z = H_s$, and establishing the key variables: horizontal displacement $u(z, t)$, acceleration $a(z, t)$, shear modulus profile $G(z)$, and the reference shear wave velocity $c_o$ at the base. For a time-harmonic input, I can substitute the frequency-domain representation into the governing equation, which simplifies it to an ordinary differential equation in terms of the amplitude function and angular frequency.\n\nNow I'm setting up the boundary conditions for this system. At the free surface, shear stress vanishes, giving a zero-gradient condition on displacement. At the base, the displacement must match the prescribed ground motion. For power-law shear modulus profiles where the modulus approaches zero at the surface, the free surface condition is automatically satisfied regardless of the displacement gradient.\n\nThe regularity of the solution at the free surface becomes the key constraint determining the integration constants. When I move to the frequency domain, the acceleration transfer function has the same spatial form as the displacement transfer function since both involve the same $-\\omega^2$ scaling factor.\n\nFor the uniform shear stiffness case where $G(z) = G_o$, the problem reduces to a classical wave equation with wavenumber $k = \\omega/c_s$, and I need to apply the boundary conditions at the free surface and base to solve for the transfer function. BC at $z = 0$ yields the closed-form displacement profile^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The trigonometric solution for a uniform elastic layer is textbook material, derivable in closed form from the second-order linear ODE with constant coefficients. The result is universally reproduced in the literature [DOI:10.1785/BSSA0660041293][WEB:https://www.resolutionmineeis.us/documents/schnabel-lysmer-seed-1972].]\n\n$$U(z, \\omega) = U_g \\frac{\\cos\\!\\left[k(H_s - z)\\right]}{\\cos(k H_s)}$$\n\nThe corresponding acceleration transfer function has the same spatial form:\n\n$$H_a(z, \\omega) = \\frac{\\cos\\!\\left[k(H_s - z)\\right]}{\\cos(k H_s)}$$\n\nwhere $H_s - z$ represents the depth from the surface to the point of interest. At the surface, the amplification becomes $|H_a(H_s, \\omega)| = 1/|\\cos(kH_s)|$, which diverges at resonance frequencies where $kH_s = (2n - 1)\\pi/2$. Now I'm moving on to consider the case of a linearly varying shear modulus profile.\n\nFor the Gibson deposit, the shear modulus increases linearly from zero at the surface to $G_o$ at depth $H_s$. Using the depth coordinate $\\eta = H_s - z$, I can reformulate the governing equation in terms of this new variable, which transforms the ODE into a form that depends on the product $k_o^2 H_s$.\n\nWith the substitution $s = 2k_o\\sqrt{H_s \\eta}$, this becomes Bessel's equation of order zero, and the physically meaningful solution that remains regular at the free surface is the Bessel function $J_0(s)$.\n\nApplying the base boundary condition $U(z=0) = U_g$ determines the amplitude constant, yielding the transfer function in terms of depth $z$ measured from the base. This gives the ratio of surface motion to base motion as the quotient of two Bessel functions evaluated at different arguments—one accounting for the depth-dependent path through the soil column, the other for the total column height.\n\nNow I'm moving to power-law shear modulus profiles, which generalize the uniform case and admit closed-form Bessel solutions across a range of exponents. The modulus decreases with depth according to a power law, parameterized by an exponent $m$ between zero and two, where the reference modulus $G_o$ is evaluated at the base.\n\nThrough a depth-based coordinate transformation, the governing differential equation reduces to Bessel's equation with order determined by the exponent $m$. The physically relevant solution is the one that remains finite at the surface, which corresponds to selecting the regular Bessel function component while suppressing the singular one.\n\nThis gives the transfer function as a ratio of Bessel functions evaluated at the surface and at depth, scaled by a power-law depth dependence that captures how the impedance varies with position in the soil column. The limiting behavior at $m \\to 0$ formally recovers the trigonometric result, though the comparison requires care since the uniform case applies a zero-stress boundary condition at the surface rather than a regularity condition, making the correspondence formal rather than exact without adjusting the boundary condition treatment. Now I'm moving to natural frequencies, which are determined by the poles of the surface transfer function—equivalently, by finding where the denominator zeros out. This is standard in structural dynamics, and for the uniform stratum case, the natural frequencies follow a specific formula. For the linear velocity profile, the natural frequencies are determined by the zeros of the Bessel function $J_0$, with the $n$-th frequency given by $\\omega_n = j_{0,n}c_o/(2H_s)$ where $j_{0,1} \\approx 2.405$, $j_{0,2} \\approx 5.520$, and $j_{0,3} \\approx 8.654$ are the first three zeros. The corresponding mode shapes take the form $\\phi_n(\\eta) = J_0(j_{0,n}\\sqrt{\\eta/H_s})$ with $\\eta = H_s - z$.\n\nFor a general power-law velocity profile, this generalizes to $\\omega_n = j_{\\nu,n}(2-m)c_o/(2H_s)$ where $j_{\\nu,n}$ is the $n$-th zero of $J_\\nu$ with order $\\nu = (1-m)/(2-m)$, derived by setting the denominator $J_\\nu[2k_o H_s/(2-m)] = 0$ and solving for the frequency.\n\nNow I'm extending this to include material damping by introducing a complex shear modulus $G^*(z) = G(z)(1 + 2i\\xi)$ where $\\xi$ is the hysteretic damping ratio, which translates to a complex wavenumber $k^* \\approx k(1 - i\\xi)$ for small damping values. This is the standard approach for incorporating frequency-dependent damping in the frequency domain.\n\nThe equivalent-linear method then iteratively refines the shear modulus and damping at each layer using modulus reduction and damping curves until the computed strain amplitude matches the assumed properties, with the closed-form solutions remaining valid within each iteration since the soil behaves as a linear viscoelastic material at each step.\n\nNow I'm identifying the five key parameters that control these closed-form solutions: the stratum thickness, the base shear wave velocity, the stiffness profile exponent, the damping ratio, and the impedance contrast at the base. I'm defining the kinematic relationship for shear strain as the vertical gradient of horizontal displacement, which is a fundamental continuum mechanics principle that holds directly for this 1D problem. In the frequency domain, this becomes a derivative of the displacement amplitude with respect to depth.\n\nNow I'm deriving a model-independent closed-form expression by integrating the equation of motion vertically from an arbitrary depth to the free surface, applying the boundary condition that shear stress vanishes at the surface. This integral representation is exact within the 1D framework and connects shear strain directly to the acceleration field. of $G(z)$. When the shear modulus is constant, the strain formula simplifies to depend only on the shear wave velocity, and I can verify this matches the frequency-domain result by differentiating the harmonic displacement solution with respect to depth. The strain transfer function follows directly from dividing by base acceleration, giving a spatial dependence in sine rather than cosine—this means maximum strain occurs at the base while the free surface experiences zero strain, sharing the same resonant frequencies as the acceleration transfer function.\n\nNow I'm working through the linear shear modulus profile case, where the displacement involves Bessel functions and I need to differentiate with respect to depth using the standard Bessel function identities. The geometric amplification factor $\\sqrt{H_s/(H_s - z)}$ captures how strain intensifies toward the surface as shear modulus approaches zero, creating a singularity that's physically regularized by nonlinearity and damping in real materials. Now I'm moving into the power-law profile case...\n\nFor a generalized power-law shear modulus with exponent $m$ between 0 and 2, I can express the displacement using Bessel functions, then differentiate to get strain using the recurrence relations...\n\nWhen I apply the Bessel recurrence formula and substitute the specific relationship between the exponent and Bessel order, the $J_\\nu$ terms cancel out algebraically, leaving me with a clean single Bessel term of order $\\nu - 1$ for the strain expression.\n\nNow I'm writing out the full form of the strain response function in terms of the Bessel function ratio, where the argument depends on the depth-dependent scaling with the exponent $(2-m)/2$. Let me verify this holds at the special case $m = 1$, which should give $\\nu = 0$ and reduce the Bessel function to a simpler form.\n\nAt $m = 1$, the formula simplifies nicely—the Bessel function indices collapse to $J_{-1}$ and $J_0$, and using the identity $J_{-1} = -J_1$, I can show that the general expression recovers exactly the linear-profile result I derived earlier. This algebraic consistency confirms the formula is correct.\n\nNow I'm moving into how the strain actually varies with depth, which involves understanding the interplay between the modal structure of the acceleration field and how the shear modulus changes with depth. For a uniform layer, the strain modes take the form of sine functions with depth-dependent arguments. are 90 degrees out of phase with the acceleration mode shapes $\\cos[k_n(H_s - z)]$; the $n$-th natural mode contributes maximum strain at mid-depth (where the mode sine is largest) relative to the acceleration mode, which peaks at the surface.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The modal structure of the strain field follows directly from differentiating the modal acceleration field. The orthogonality and phase relationship between cosine (acceleration) and sine (strain) modes is a standard result for the uniform shear beam [DOI:10.1785/BSSA0660041293].]\n\nFor profiles with $G(z) \\to 0$ at the surface (i.e., $m > 0$), the power-law term $(H_s/(H_s-z))^{(2m-1)/2}$ governs how strain varies with depth near the free surface. When $m > 1/2$, the exponent becomes positive and strain amplifies toward the surface regardless of the Bessel function behavior, whereas for $m < 1/2$ the algebraic prefactor decays toward the surface and can partially counteract the Bessel contribution, though the net effect depends on frequency-dependent interactions.^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The depth-dependent amplification follows directly from the algebraic prefactor in the strain formula, and the threshold at $m = 1/2$ is exact. The qualitative behavior for $m < 1/2$ is less certain since the relative magnitudes of competing terms vary with frequency.] The formula for fundamental-mode shear strain uses the participation factor and wave number to capture the depth-dependent strain profile, with the key parameters being the participation factor of 4/π for a uniform shear beam and the corresponding wave number. For layered deposits, higher modes contribute more significantly to strain than to acceleration because the strain spatial patterns have shorter wavelengths at each mode. I need to verify that every prose paragraph in my manuscript has exactly one audit footnote at the end. Display math blocks themselves don't need footnotes, but any prose introducing them does. Let me scan through and identify which sections are missing their confidence ratings, then add them systematically. I'm working through the footnote placement systematically—each prose block needs its own annotation, and I need to be careful about how display equations relate to their introductory text. When a sentence ends with a colon before a display equation, that's a continuation, so the footnote should come after the equation. But standalone prose blocks that follow equations need their own footnotes. Let me restructure the manuscript to make these relationships clear and ensure every explanatory passage is properly marked. I'm working through the manuscript structure now, identifying each prose paragraph that needs a footnote annotation. I'm counting the sections systematically—the introduction covering notation and the physical model, the lead-ins to each display equation, the boundary condition explanation, and the transfer function descriptions—making sure I capture every paragraph that requires a confidence footnote before I write out the full clean version. Now I'm showing how this recovers the uniform case, then deriving the natural frequencies for the deposit and their corresponding mode shapes. For the power-law profile, the frequencies depend on Bessel function zeros, and I'm noting how material damping fits into the framework through the equivalent-linear method, which relies on five key parameters controlling the system's behavior. Moving into the strain calculations, I'm expressing the engineering shear strain at depth in both time and frequency domains. Now I'm working through the strain transfer function for the linear profile case, then moving into power-law profiles where I need to differentiate with respect to depth and establish the general form with the dimensionless parameter eta. I'm verifying the results at the special case m=1 and examining how shear strain varies with depth, particularly for profiles where the shear modulus approaches zero at the surface.\n\nI'm continuing with the fundamental-mode amplitude and the specific coefficient Gamma_1, then addressing how non-uniform profiles affect the overall behavior. Given the scope of this derivation, I'm consolidating related concepts into fewer but more substantive paragraphs rather than fragmenting the exposition across many short blocks, aiming for a manuscript around 3500-4500 words with comprehensive footnotes integrated throughout.\n\nBefore finalizing, I need to verify the dimensional consistency of the general power-law strain formula—checking that the acceleration-to-velocity-frequency ratio yields the correct units for strain amplitude. s^2]/[m/s · s^{-1}] = [m/s^2] · [s^2/m] = dimensionless ✓\n- $(H_s/(H_s-z))^{(2m-1)/2}$ - dimensionless ✓\n- $J_{\\nu-1}/H_s J_\\nu$ - [1/m] · dimensionless/dimensionless = [1/m]? \n\nWait, that gives 1/m, but strain should be dimensionless.\n\nLet me recheck. From my earlier derivation:\n\n$\\Gamma(\\xi) = \\frac{U_g k_o H_s^{m/2}\\xi^{(1-2m)/2}}{H_s^{(1-m)/2}}J_{\\nu-1}(\\zeta)/J_\\nu(\\zeta_H)$\n\nNow I'm working through the dimensional analysis of the numerator terms. Combining the powers of $H_s$ with the other length scales, the exponents work out to sum to zero, confirming the expression is dimensionless before accounting for the Bessel function ratio. is dimensionless. Now in my formula:\n\n$-\\frac{A_g}{c_o\\omega}\\left(\\frac{H_s}{H_s-z}\\right)^{(2m-1)/2}\\frac{J_{\\nu-1}}{H_s J_\\nu}$\n\n$A_g/c_o\\omega$ has units of $[m/s^2]/([m/s][1/s]) = [m/s^2][s^2/m] = $ dimensionless... wait:\n\n$c_o$ has units of m/s, $\\omega$ has units of rad/s = 1/s\n\n$c_o\\omega$ has units of [m/s][1/s] = m/s^2\n\n$A_g/(c_o\\omega)$ has units of $[m/s^2]/[m/s^2]$ = dimensionless ✓\n\n$(H_s/(H_s-z))^{(2m-1)/2}$ is dimensionless ✓\n\nNow checking the Bessel function ratio—if $J_{\\nu-1}/(H_s J_\\nu)$ gives me 1/m, then the whole expression ends up with the wrong units. Let me trace back through the original derivation where $\\Gamma = C_1 k_o H_s^{m/2}\\xi^{(1-2m)/2}J_{\\nu-1}(\\zeta)$ and $C_1 = U_g/[H_s^{(1-m)/2}J_\\nu(\\zeta_H)]$ to find where the dimensional error is creeping in. I'm verifying that all the dimensionless components check out—the ratio terms and Bessel functions are all dimensionless, so Γ itself is dimensionless as expected. Now I'm converting to the depth coordinate η measured from the surface, and accounting for the sign change when switching from the z-coordinate system to the η-coordinate system.\n\nSubstituting the definitions for $U_g$ and $k_o$ in terms of the acceleration amplitude $A_g$, angular frequency $\\omega$, and wave speed $c_o$, I'm simplifying the expression down to a cleaner form with the depth-dependent ratio and Bessel function ratio. I'm verifying the algebraic equivalence between these two forms by checking the exponent manipulation—flipping the fraction in the ratio inverts the exponent sign, confirming both expressions are indeed the same.\n\nNow I see the issue with my earlier derivation: there shouldn't be an $H_s$ term in the denominator outside the Bessel functions. The corrected formula has the normalized depth ratio raised to the power $(1-2m)/2$, with the Bessel function ratio comparing the argument at depth $z$ to the argument at the surface.\n\nLet me check this holds for the $m=1$ case: the exponent becomes $-1/2$, giving $\\sqrt{H_s/(H_s-z)}$, and the Bessel indices shift to $J_{-1}/J_0$, which simplifies to $-J_1/J_0$ since $J_{-1} = -J_1$.\n\nNow for $m=0$ with the uniform profile, the exponent is $1/2$ instead, and I need to use the explicit forms for the half-integer Bessel functions—specifically $J_{-1/2}$ and $J_{1/2}$ in terms of trigonometric functions. This gives me a ratio of cosine to sine terms after substituting the Bessel function definitions and simplifying the square roots.\n\nHowever, this result doesn't match what I expect for uniform shear with the standard stress-free boundary condition at the surface. The discrepancy arises because the Bessel approach with $m=0$ actually imposes a regularity condition rather than zero stress, which is a fundamentally different physical constraint that produces a different solution.\n\nI need to correct my manuscript claim about recovering the uniform-layer result in the $m \\to 0$ limit—that's not accurate. The Bessel formula only applies for $m \\in (0, 2)$, so the $m=0$ case requires special treatment and can't be obtained as a simple limit of the general formula.\n\nNow looking at the acceleration transfer function specifically, I'm checking whether the Bessel formula recovers the correct uniform result when $m=0$. Working through the algebra with $\\nu = 1/2$ and the Bessel function identity, I get $\\sin(k\\eta)/\\sin(kH_s)$, but the actual uniform solution should give $\\cos(k\\eta)/\\cos(kH_s)$—these don't match, confirming the formula breaks down at $m=0$.\n\nThe issue is that the power-law model with $m=0$ satisfies different boundary conditions than the uniform case, so I need to revise my manuscript to clarify that $m=0$ requires separate treatment since the free surface singularity vanishes there.\n\nNow I'm ready to write out the complete manuscript carefully, making sure every prose section has a confidence footnote, all math is properly formatted, and citations are included throughout.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":70330,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":70330,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8dbc7cde-e70a-4bb0-94b0-45fd1a651a76","uuid":"d407688e-1cf0-4325-a32e-6130b79502bf"}
